<rml>
<head>
	<title>Mask image</title>
	<meta name="folio-size" content="1190x714"/>
	<link type="text/rcss" href="/folio/folio.rcss"/>
	<style>
		/* Every shape of mask-image, side by side with the same tile unmasked.

		   A mask is a render layer: the subtree is drawn into an offscreen target, the mask
		   decorator is drawn into another, and the two are composited. So this is really a test
		   of the layer path, which is the half of the renderer a screenshot of an ordinary
		   document never touches. It is also the only place text is drawn through a layer rather
		   than straight to the target, which is why every tile has words in it.

		   Only a Surface world document has layers. In-scene and after-tonemap documents record a
		   draw list, which has no offscreen target to composite through, and every tile here will
		   render unmasked with one warning. That is the expected result there, not a failure.

		   Read it by comparing each tile to the control on its left. A mask that does nothing
		   looks exactly like the control; a mask that composites to nothing leaves a hole. */
		body {
			font-family: Inter;
			font-size: 18dp;
			color: #e8ecf2;
			background-color: #0d1015;
			width: 100%;
			height: 100%;
			box-sizing: border-box;
			padding: 24dp;
			display: flex;
			flex-direction: column;
		}

		h1 {
			font-size: 22dp;
			font-weight: 600;
			color: #f0c674;
			margin-bottom: 4dp;
		}

		.note {
			font-size: 14dp;
			color: #7b8494;
			margin-bottom: 20dp;
		}

		.grid {
			display: flex;
			flex-wrap: wrap;
			flex: 1 1 auto;
		}

		.cell {
			width: 274dp;
			margin: 0 12dp 20dp 0;
		}

		.label {
			font-size: 13dp;
			font-weight: 600;
			letter-spacing: 0.4dp;
			color: #7b8494;
			margin-bottom: 6dp;
		}

		/* The tile is deliberately busy. A flat colour would hide a mask that is subtly wrong,
		   and text is what shows an offscreen target being composited at the wrong offset. */
		.tile {
			height: 150dp;
			padding: 14dp;
			box-sizing: border-box;
			background-color: #182030;
			border: 1dp #ffffff1f;
			border-radius: 8dp;
			display: flex;
			flex-direction: column;
			justify-content: space-between;
		}

		.tile .word { font-size: 26dp; font-weight: 600; color: #ffffff; }
		.tile .row { display: flex; align-items: center; }
		.tile .swatch {
			width: 22dp; height: 22dp;
			border-radius: 4dp;
			background-color: #4ea1ff;
			margin-right: 8dp;
		}
		.tile .bar {
			height: 8dp;
			border-radius: 4dp;
			background-color: #4ea1ff;
			flex: 0 0 auto;
			width: 60%;
		}

		/* A mask reads its own alpha, so every gradient here has to end at a transparent stop.
		   One that runs between two opaque colours masks nothing and is the easiest way to write
		   this test wrong. */
		.linear     { mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #ffffff 0%, #ffffff00 100%); }
		.radial     { mask-image: radial-gradient(circle closest-side at 50% 50%, #ffffff 40%, #ffffff00 100%); }
		.conic      { mask-image: conic-gradient(from 0deg, #ffffff 0%, #ffffff00 50%, #ffffff 100%); }
		.repeating  { mask-image: repeating-linear-gradient(0deg, #ffffff 0dp, #ffffff 10dp, #ffffff00 10dp, #ffffff00 20dp); }
		.vertical   { mask-image: vertical-gradient(#ffffff #ffffff00); }

		/* An opaque mask covering the whole element. It must look identical to the control:
		   anything else is the compositing step itself being wrong, not the mask shape. */
		.opaque     { mask-image: vertical-gradient(#ffffff #ffffff); }
	</style>
</head>
<body>
	<h1>mask-image</h1>
	<p class="note">Each tile is masked by the decorator named above it. The control has no mask
	and is what the rest are read against.</p>

	<div class="grid">
		<div class="cell">
			<div class="label">CONTROL, NO MASK</div>
			<div class="tile">
				<div class="word">Control</div>
				<div class="row"><div class="swatch"></div><div class="bar"></div></div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="cell">
			<div class="label">OPAQUE MASK</div>
			<div class="tile opaque">
				<div class="word">Opaque</div>
				<div class="row"><div class="swatch"></div><div class="bar"></div></div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="cell">
			<div class="label">LINEAR-GRADIENT</div>
			<div class="tile linear">
				<div class="word">Linear</div>
				<div class="row"><div class="swatch"></div><div class="bar"></div></div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="cell">
			<div class="label">VERTICAL-GRADIENT</div>
			<div class="tile vertical">
				<div class="word">Vertical</div>
				<div class="row"><div class="swatch"></div><div class="bar"></div></div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="cell">
			<div class="label">RADIAL-GRADIENT</div>
			<div class="tile radial">
				<div class="word">Radial</div>
				<div class="row"><div class="swatch"></div><div class="bar"></div></div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="cell">
			<div class="label">CONIC-GRADIENT</div>
			<div class="tile conic">
				<div class="word">Conic</div>
				<div class="row"><div class="swatch"></div><div class="bar"></div></div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="cell">
			<div class="label">REPEATING-LINEAR</div>
			<div class="tile repeating">
				<div class="word">Repeat</div>
				<div class="row"><div class="swatch"></div><div class="bar"></div></div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="cell">
			<div class="label">MASK OVER OPACITY</div>
			<div class="tile linear" style="opacity: 0.6;">
				<div class="word">Stacked</div>
				<div class="row"><div class="swatch"></div><div class="bar"></div></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</body>
</rml>
